{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788311827,"nonce":"LsloSA5iZ8ln1Km2AW+o16+FhLI=","title":"**Filed mid-run (m1-20260901, tick 47,365). p1 because the M1 contract grades T6 on this exact field.** Evan spotted it from the window: *\"the beds are assigned?? otherwise they wouldn't be sleeping right now.\"* He is right — all three colonists were asleep in their own beds at the moment the read was taken.","message":"`room {id:38}` on a colonist-built barracks:\n\n```json\n{\"id\":38,\"role\":\"Barracks\",\"cells\":35,\"proper\":true,\"open_roof_cells\":0,\n \"beds\":[\n   {\"id\":46166,\"def\":\"Bed\",\"at\":[105,141],\"for_prisoners\":false,\"medical\":false,\n    \"owners\":[{\"id\":313,\"name\":\"Wouter\"}]},\n   {\"id\":46186,\"def\":\"Bed\",\"at\":[107,141],\"for_prisoners\":false,\"medical\":false,\n    \"owners\":[{\"id\":310,\"name\":\"Lacey\"}]},\n   {\"id\":46139,\"def\":\"Bed\",\"at\":[109,141],\"for_prisoners\":false,\"medical\":false,\n    \"owners\":[{\"id\":323,\"name\":\"Jimmy\"}]}],\n \"owners_total\":0,\n \"owners\":[]}\n```\n\nThree beds, three distinct owners, one per bed — and `owners_total: 0` with `owners: []` in the same response. The per-bed `owners` arrays are right; the room-level rollup is wrong. `rooms` (the plural verb) reports the same `owners_total: 0`.\n\nCorroboration from outside the verb: `digest.colonists` at the same tick reads `Wouter: sleeping. barracks`, `Lacey: sleeping. barracks`, `Jimmy: sleeping. barracks`, and the game had already cleared `Alert_NeedColonistBeds` (journal seq 94, tick 12,464). Ownership was never issued by the agent — no `assign` call was made in the run — so the game auto-claimed the beds as the pawns went to sleep, which is the normal vanilla path.\n\n\n`664e9b9` comment #8's thrive table, row **T6**:\n\n\u003e | T6 | barracks | `room` on a colonist-built room | `role:\"Barracks\"`, `proper:true`, `open_roof_cells:0`; `beds` count \u003e= roster and **`owners_total` == roster** |\n\nEvery other clause of T6 already passes on room 38. The one clause that fails, fails because the field is broken, not because the colony is. A run graded today scores T6 FAIL on a barracks that satisfies it completely — and the failure is silent, because `owners_total: 0` is exactly what an unassigned barracks would also report. There is no way to tell \"nobody owns these beds\" from \"the rollup is broken\" without reading `beds[].owners` and noticing they disagree.\n\nThe same shape threatens any acceptance suite that asserts on `owners_total`, per [[acceptance-suites-must-prove-shapes]] — an assertion of `owners_total == 0` on a fresh room passes today for the wrong reason.\n\n\n`Source/AutoRimmer/PlaceVerbs.cs:225–246` builds both fields from a single iteration of `room.Owners`:\n\n```csharp\nvar owners = new List\u003cobject\u003e();\nint ownerTotal = 0;\ntry {\n    foreach (var p in room.Owners) {\n        ownerTotal++;\n        if (owners.Count \u003e= OwnerCap || p == null) continue;\n        owners.Add(...);\n    }\n} catch { }\n```\n\nSo `ownerTotal` is 0 iff `room.Owners` yielded nothing — or iff the enumeration threw and was swallowed by the bare `catch {}`, which would be indistinguishable from an empty room. Note the `beds[]` block at PlaceVerbs.cs:173–192 builds its `owners` from a different route and gets the right answer, so the two disagree about the same three beds.\n\n**Which of those it is has NOT been investigated** — Evan's call, so the run could keep playing. Two candidates a refining agent should separate before touching anything: `Verse/Room.Owners` may only yield for the single-owner bedroom case and deliberately yield nothing for a Barracks; or the enumeration is throwing and the bare `catch` is eating it. The comment above the loop already says `Room.Owners` \"re-enters `ContainedAndAdjacentThings` three times\", which is a hint that its semantics were looked at once and are worth looking at again.\n\n\n1. `room` and `rooms` report `owners_total` and `owners` consistent with the `beds[].owners` in the same response. On room 38's shape — three non-medical, non-prisoner beds owned by three distinct colonists — `owners_total` is 3 and `owners` names all three.\n2. The two routes cannot silently disagree again: either the rollup is derived from the same per-bed data the `beds[]` block uses, or a mismatch between them emits a `Journal.EmitWarning` naming the room. A bare `catch {}` around an owner enumeration is replaced by one that records that it fired — an exception here currently presents as a legitimately empty room.\n3. Cover the cases that are genuinely 0 so the fix does not just hardcode agreement: an empty barracks with unowned beds, a medical bed, a prisoner bed, and a bedroom with one owner. `RoomRoleWorker_Bedroom.IsBedroom`'s own distinction between `bed_emptyCountsForBarracks` and owned beds is the vocabulary to test against.\n4. `accept/\u003cid\u003e-room-owners.py` on a bench: build a 3-bed room, advance until the pawns claim beds, assert `owners_total == 3` and that it equals the count of distinct pawns across `beds[].owners`. Assert the unowned case reads 0 for the right reason.\n5. `664e9b9` gets a comment noting that T6's `owners_total` clause was unreadable for the duration of run `m1-20260901`, with the substitute read the run used instead (distinct pawns across `beds[].owners`), so the run's own T6 verdict is auditable.\n\n\nGrades T6 off `beds[].owners` — three beds, three distinct owners — and says so in the summary rather than quoting a field it knows is broken.","files":null}]}